\f1\fs22 The hover-copt landed with tax-you abruptness on the avenue, scattering startled pedestrians and coating them in a wheeze of fumes and burned aerosolised oils. Its door slid back and Dunhilton alighted cradling a therapy piglet with performative tenderness. The animal, pink and dopey-eyed, nuzzled against his navy suit jacket.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hatchet & Grimes occupied a discreet shopfront of a stately old light-industry tower on the \'93ground\'94 floor\'97which is to say, as close to the ground without tempting the fates of the Layer on a windy day. Its entrance sat between a wealth management firm and a private dentist that specialised in cosmetic procedures for those who\'92d said the wrong thing at the wrong time. The establishment\'92s brass plaque, polished to a gleam that suggested either pride or obsessive-compulsive disorder, announced: Executive Stress Relief Since the 19th Century.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Inside, an attendant in burgundy livery\'97the sort that made one think of Victorian gamekeepers who\'92d routinely strangle at least one poacher before breakfast\'97greeted Dunhilton with practiced deference.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Ah, Mr. Dunhilton. Always a pleasure. And I see you\'92ve brought a guest.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton handed over the piglet. The attendant received it as if accepting a ceremonial offering, cradling it against his chest.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Therapy animal,\'94 Dunhilton explained. \'93One of my assistants kept it under her desk. Something about emotional support and workplace wellness. Thought we\'92d give the little fellow some exercise.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93How thoughtful, sir. Is it just the piglet today, or could I interest you in some accompaniments or side-dishes?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton barely looked at the backlit menu board looming from the ceiling behind the counter. It glowed with the cheerful menace of a fast-food franchise that had made a hard pivot into recreational cruelty.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What can we interest you in today? Our Wolf Pack Combo is the special\'97six grey rabbits, four black ducks, and a rotating selection of seasonal game birds\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Is there a partridge in a pear tree?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The piglet snorted in the attendant\'92s arms, as if getting the joke.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It comes with a free upgrade to axes with ergonomic grips, and complimentary side of beef jerky,\'94 the attendant said as the piglet twisted in his arms.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I had my heart set on going all the way with the Apex Predator Combo today,\'94 Dunhilton said, as if his heart had been set in concrete.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The attendant\'92s smile widened by perhaps two millimetres\'97enough to suggest genuine pleasure, but not so much as to seem unprofessional. He swallowed an unbidden image of a brothel madam stuffing cash into her till. \'93Excellent choice, sir. Might I suggest you top it off with a peacock?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton grunted assent.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92ll just put on your ex-wife\'92s credit card, as usual?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton sneered. \'93Of course.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The attendant made notes on a small tablet. \'93Your usual lane?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Please.\'94 Dunhilton liked the lane three-quarters of the way down.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92ll have it all staged immediately, sir.\'94 The attendant took the piglet through a door marked STAFF ONLY.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton took his spot at the head of his preferred lane in the throwing room. He felt he knew every scar on the polished wooden wall, exposed in bright relief by the kind of lighting that suggested either a wine cellar or an operating theatre, depending on your temperament. Across the wall, a random arrangement of white dinner plates sat balancing precariously in cradles. Below the plates a glass-fronted channel, perhaps a metre wide, ran the length of the visible wall. At both ends it disappeared behind heavy, dark curtains.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>As the attendant emerged and took his place at the front counter, Gerry Hardman arrived. Dunhilton could not hide his delight; Gerry looked like he was arriving too early to a birthday party for someone he didn\'92t like.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Gerry!\'94 Dunhilton clapped him on the shoulder with enthusiasm. \'93Glad you could make it. Thought we\'92d have a proper catch-up. Away from the office. Somewhere we can relax.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry\'92s tablet buzzed. He swiped, and frowned almost as much as a brick might.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Just got something you\'92ll want to see,\'94 he said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93In a moment. First, you\'92re throwing.\'94 Dunhilton raised his eyebrows at the rack of axes.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92m fine, thanks.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Nonsense. Team building. Stress relief. Get ahead of all the harassment from People and Culture about work-life balance.\'94 He selected an axe, tested its weight, then grinned.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>From behind the heavy curtains at the far end came a muffled squeal, then another. Something was happening back there\'97something involving the Apex Predator Combo and probably not much in the way of dignity.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The therapy piglet shot out from behind the curtain like it had discovered religion and needed to share the apocalyptic news. It bolted down the glass-fronted race, pink legs pumping, eyes significantly less dopey.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton threw on pure instinct.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The blade sailed past every plate on the wall and buried itself in the timber with a solid thunk. The piglet was already gone, vanishing behind the curtain at the near end.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Damn, he\'92s fast,\'94 Dunhilton muttered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>More sounds of animal alarm from behind the curtains. A rabbit burst out, froze halfway down the channel as if reconsidering its life choices, then bucked forward. A duck followed, wings bound close to its body with what looked like kitchen twine, waddling with the indignant fury of something that had been promised a pond and found a tarpit.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You should watch this,\'94 Gerry said, holding up his tablet.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Can\'92t it wait?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Not really.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton sighed, selected another axe, and took the tablet with his free hand.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The video opened mid-scene\'97phone camera footage, vertical format, the kind of shaky handheld work that suggested the cameraman was drunk and standing on someone\'92s shoulders.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The view lurched through a doorway into what looked like a derelict nightclub that had given up on life rather than tolerate smartphones. Overhead lighting barely functioned, throwing shadows that made the whole space look like it was underwater. Chandeliers hung at odd angles, their crystals so tarnished they\'92d lost the right to sparkle. Wallpaper peeled in strips that might once have been red or purple.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What is this place?\'94 Dunhilton asked, not looking up from the screen.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It\'92s the new Parliament House of the Elvi,\'94 Gerry answered. \'93Used to be a brothel called Clambake till they repurposed it.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A pigeon shot out of the curtain, fluttering uselessly with its wings tied, and hit the glass front of the channel with a dull thump before settling into a panicked run.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton snorted. \'93Parliament. Tax me sideways.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He threw the axe one-handed, still watching the video. The blade struck a plate dead centre. The plate swung free and shattered, sending shards into the channel. A guinea fowl that had just emerged squawked and reversed direction, disappearing back behind the curtain.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The camera panned\'97badly\'97across a crowd that looked like a costume shop had been hit by a tornado and the survivors had formed a cult. Leather jackets next to army surplus, sequinned jumpsuits catching the dim light like disco balls having nervous breakdowns. A photobomber jumped into view, wearing a white jumpsuit with enough rhinestones to be visible from orbit.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>In the centre of the crowd, a figure stood on\'97or in\'97something elevated. The camera zoomed in\'97amateur work, all pixelation and motion blur. Dunhilton tried to make sense of \'85 mosaic tiles \'85 taps that had probably been gold once.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Is that a spa bath?\'94 Dunhilton\'92s beliefs of how the world worked were shunted into new dimensions.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry nodded, eyes wide and eyebrows raised so high, his hairline retreated to the back of his head.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93My fellow Elvi,\'94 the figure standing in the bath said, voice tinny through the tablet speakers. \'93Praise the Dawkins.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The crowd roared back something unintelligible. The audio clipped, distorting into static.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Two rabbits burst out together, one chasing the other. Behind them, a peacock emerged with as much dignity as a flightless bird with bound wings could muster, which is to say, none whatsoever.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Elvis in the tub launched into what might have been a speech, or could have been a Tonight Show host monologue. \'93I am all shook up by the honour you bestow upon me...\'94 The words kept coming\'97something about bunny love and seditious minds and molly water. To Dunhilton, it seemed like a rant about bestiality and drug abuse. None of it made sense, but it was more than the bad sound quality.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Gerry. Throw something.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92m fine.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It\'92s therapeutic.\'94 Dunhilton\'92s voice carried the insistence that therapy was mandatory.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry set down his tablet, selected an axe with the enthusiasm of an accountant choosing between two identical staplers, and threw with the kind of deliberate incompetence that suggested he\'92d calculated exactly where it would miss. The blade clattered uselessly against the wall above the plates and tumbled down into the channel.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Something squealed. Gerry winced.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The peacock reversed direction with impressive speed for something so overdressed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry retrieved his tablet and resumed scrolling through emails. A faint smell\'97organic, distinctly mammalian, with notes of iron and copper\'97drifted from the channel.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton kept watching. The High Elvis was still talking, words tumbling out. \'93...it\'92s time we reclaimed our sense of pride, and our self-determination. It\'92s time for deeds and actions.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93He\'92s inciting sedition,\'94 Dunhilton muttered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Animals ran the race more frequently now\'97three rabbits, a duck, two pigeons and a single partridge in rapid succession. The channel became a highway of panic. Animals reversed, colliding with newcomers. The squealing from behind the curtains rose.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Our spirits will rise from the shadows of the Ghetto,\'94 the spa bath Elvis continued, \'93and even from the Pale too, may their hopes and dreams shine brightly.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Pale. That got Dunhilton\'92s attention. If the Elvi were planning to spread into The Pale, that was a serious problem. He threw another axe without looking. A plate shattered. A rabbit attempting to return the way it came reconsidered and fled back toward the far curtain.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The smell was getting worse. Gerry\'92s face remained expressionless, while shifting his weight between feet.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>On screen, the Elvis kept building his rhetoric about cleaning up the Ghetto, about dignity and self-respect. The camera work got shakier. The audio peaked and distorted as they chanted\'97something about Elvis, something about Dawkins.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Then: \'93Bless the Holy Momma!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton froze, tablet halfway to his face.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The camera swung wildly\'97amateur hour continuing\'97and climbed up to catch something elevated at the back of the room. Two chairs\'97no\'97thrones. Fake and kitsch, for sure, but Dunhilton recognised ceremony and power when he saw it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>On the right sat an embodiment of absurdity and poor taste. Gold fabric, so much gold fabric. Red velvet cape that went on forever. Rhinestones that caught every photon and weaponised them. Across the chest, a sash proclaimed something in letters the camera couldn\'92t quite resolve through the glare. On the head: a crown made of what looked like\'97Dunhilton squinted\'97melted plastic figurines?\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sunglasses. Enormous sunglasses, lenses so dark they could have been portals to a tax-free universe.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Who the tax is that supposed to be?\'94 Dunhilton asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Not sure,\'94 Gerry said, breathing shallowly through his mouth. \'93Some kind of priest?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The camera held on the ridiculous figure for another moment\'97everything about him screamed either holy relic or practical joke, impossible to tell which\'97then panned left.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>To a woman in a green dress.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The camera focused\'97finally, some competent camerawork\'97on the woman\'92s face. Tired. Blonde hair. One hand resting on the throne\'92s arm, the other on her belly.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton\'92s face suffered a succession of expressions in quick succession: confusion, recognition, fury, and finally a frisson of satisfaction.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That\'92s\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Sarah Jacobson,\'94 Gerry confirmed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93And they\'92re worshipping her.\'94 Dunhilton\'92s voice had gone very quiet. The kind of quiet that preceded either violence or policy.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The channel was chaos now\'97animals running in both directions, colliding, reversing, squealing. The therapy piglet shot out again.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton set down the tablet, selected the heaviest axe from the rack, and took his time choosing his moment.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The piglet slowed, confused by the traffic ahead.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton threw.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The axe flew in a perfect arc. It struck a plate directly above the piglet with enough force to not just shatter it but bury the axe in the wall. Shards and jagged pieces exploded down.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The therapy piglet looked up.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The axe fell loose under its own weight.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The squealing that followed was, in Dunhilton\'92s professional opinion, the most satisfying sound he\'92d heard all week. \'93Anyone for bacon?\'94 he asked Gerry, and threw one more axe.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry tried to move upwind.
